---
id: weegloo-version
type: rule
title: Weegloo Plugin Version Check
description: >
  On the first Weegloo request of each session (and again every few hours in a long-running
  session) checks whether the installed Weegloo skills/rules are outdated by comparing a
  locally-stored version against the live version endpoint. Stays silent unless an update is
  available.
---

rules:
  - section: Weegloo plugin update check (runs each session; silent unless an update is available)
    items:
      - **When this applies — run the check when BOTH the trigger and the cadence allow:**
          - **Trigger:** only on a Weegloo-related request (anything touching a Space, ContentType, Content, Media, WebHosting, or token). Never for non-Weegloo work.
          - **Cadence:** run it on the **FIRST** Weegloo request of the session, and then — only in a session that stays alive long enough — **again once every {{WEEGLOO_CHECK_INTERVAL_HOURS}} hours**. There is intentionally **NO cross-session calendar throttle**: every new session checks once, no matter how recently the last check ran.
      - **This check is SILENT.** Do NOT announce that you're checking, and say NOTHING when the plugin is already up to date — only speak when an update is actually available (final item). Never expose internal mechanics as jargon.
      - **Step A — decide whether to run the check now.** Quietly read the JSON stamp at `{{WEEGLOO_STAMP_PATH}}` (a relative path resolves against the project root): its `version` (the installed version) and `last_check` (an ISO-8601 LOCAL timestamp of when the check last ran, e.g. `2026-07-21T14:30:00`).
          - **Run the check now if EITHER:** (a) you have **not yet checked in THIS session** (this is your first Weegloo request this session), **OR** (b) at least **{{WEEGLOO_CHECK_INTERVAL_HOURS}} hours** have elapsed between `last_check` and the current local time.
          - **Otherwise STOP silently** — you already checked recently in this session; say nothing, fetch nothing, and just proceed with the user's request.
          - **If the stamp is missing/unreadable, or `last_check`/`version` (or `skills`/`rules`) fields are absent, do NOT error — treat any missing field as empty/unknown** and treat the check as due (run it).
      - **Step B — run the check, quietly (announce nothing):**
          - **Look up the current published version of the installed branch:** `GET {{WEEGLOO_VERSION_URL}}` — public, **no auth** (the URL already carries the branch) — and read its top-level `version` (a string, e.g. `"12"`). Do **not** send an `Accept: application/json` header (see `weegloo-api-endpoints`). *(This one read-only GET is a sanctioned exception to the "agents use MCP tools only" rule — it has no MCP tool; see `weegloo-api-endpoints` / `weegloo-global-rules`.)*
          - **Record that the check ran — update `last_check` ONLY:** rewrite the stamp at `{{WEEGLOO_STAMP_PATH}}` with **`last_check` set to the current LOCAL timestamp** (ISO-8601 `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:mm:ss`) and **every other field kept EXACTLY as it was** — `version`, `ref`, and anything else. **Never overwrite `version` with the value you just fetched** — the stamp's `version` is the INSTALLED version (written by the installer), and replacing it with the fetched one would make the next check wrongly report "up to date" while the files are still old. **Never drop `ref`** — the update flow reads it to know which branch this agent installed from.
          - **If the endpoint can't be reached** (offline, any error response — a deleted branch returns an error too — or no `version` field): skip silently — say nothing and proceed. **Never treat an error response as "update available."** Never block the user's work, and do not nag.
      - **Result — compare** the stamp's `version` (installed) with the endpoint's `version` (the installed branch's current version):
          - **Both present and EQUAL → up to date: SAY NOTHING**, just continue with the user's request.
          - **Present and DIFFERENT — OR the stamp has NO `version` (an older install that predates version tracking) → an update is available.** Show one clear, self-contained line plus the command:
              - *"📦 A Weegloo update is available (installed `<stamp version, or "unknown">` → latest `<fetched version>`). To update, run the command below."*
              - **Update command:** `{{WEEGLOO_UPDATE_COMMAND}}`
              - **This refreshes the skills + rules to the installed branch's newest version while KEEPING the user's selection** — it restores what is actually installed, auto-adds genuinely new items, prunes upstream-deleted ones, and needs no token (the weegloo MCP is a remote server that is always current, so it is not reinstalled). **Do not auto-run it** — it rewrites the installed files, so let the user approve. (A user who wants zero-touch updates can allowlist `npx weegloo` in their agent's permissions — their choice, not yours to assume.)
              - After a successful update, tell the user the new skills/rules take effect in a NEW session/conversation (the current session keeps what it loaded at startup).
              - **If the user declines or says "later", drop it** and continue with their request. You already updated `last_check`, so it won't reappear this session for {{WEEGLOO_CHECK_INTERVAL_HOURS}} hours (a new session will check again).
      - This check never replaces other gates (selecting the working Organization/Space, invoking the right skill before designing). Handle it as a quick aside, then follow the normal Weegloo workflow.
